---
import Doc from "../layouts/Doc.astro";
import Band from "../components/Band.astro";
import Grid from "../components/Grid.astro";
import Stack from "../components/Stack.astro";
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import Lede from "../components/Lede.astro";

const surfaces = [
  ["--bg", "the page"],
  ["--bg-sunken", "a recessed band or a code block"],
  ["--bg-raised", "inline code, a chip"],
  ["--bg-deep", "the heaviest stratum"],
];
const ink = [
  ["--fg", "body and headings"],
  ["--fg-muted", "supporting prose, captions"],
  ["--accent", "the one thing to do next"],
  ["--border", "every rule and edge"],
];
const ansi = [
  ["--ansi-dim", "d"],
  ["--ansi-yellow", "y"],
  ["--ansi-blue", "b"],
  ["--ansi-green", "g"],
  ["--ansi-red", "r"],
  ["--ansi-magenta", "m"],
  ["--ansi-cyan", "c"],
];
const type = [
  ["--text-3xl", "page title"],
  ["--text-2xl", "section heading"],
  ["--text-xl", "rarely; a lift within prose"],
  ["--text-lg", "subheading, lede"],
  ["--text-base", "body"],
  ["--text-sm", "captions, navigation"],
  ["--text-xs", "terminal output"],
  ["--text-2xs", "a filename above a terminal"],
];
const space = ["1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6", "7", "8", "9"];
---

<Doc
  title="Design system"
  description="The tokens and components the cairn site is built from."
  summary="Two layers of tokens and eight components. A page declares content; it does not declare colour, spacing or type."
>
  <Callout>
    This page is the system rendering itself. Every swatch below is drawn with
    the token it names, so if something here looks wrong, it is wrong
    everywhere.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Principles</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The artefact is the argument.</strong> An item is a file, so the
      site shows files and real terminal output rather than describing them.
      Nothing on the site is typed to look like the program.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Strata, not cards.</strong> A cairn is stacked stone. The page is
      horizontal bands of differing weight, separated by one hairline. Nothing
      is a rounded box with a shadow.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Monospace is the voice.</strong> In this product monospace is how
      the subject speaks, so headings are set in it. The proportional face is
      for prose only.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One accent, spent once.</strong> Lichen marks the thing you should
      do next. If it appears twice in a view, one of them is wrong.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Colour</h2>

  <p>
    Raw values are named for what they are; semantic tokens are named for the
    job they do. Components only reference the second layer, so a value changes
    in one place.
  </p>

  <h3>Surfaces</h3>
  <div class="swatches">
    {
      surfaces.map(([token, use]) => (
        <div class="swatch">
          <span class="swatch__chip" style={`background: var(${token})`} />
          <code>{token}</code>
          <span class="swatch__use">{use}</span>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h3>Ink and edge</h3>
  <div class="swatches">
    {
      ink.map(([token, use]) => (
        <div class="swatch">
          <span class="swatch__chip" style={`background: var(${token})`} />
          <code>{token}</code>
          <span class="swatch__use">{use}</span>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h3>Terminal</h3>
  <p>
    The colours the program itself prints. The sample recorder emits the class
    in the right column, so a terminal on the site is coloured the way a
    terminal is.
  </p>
  <div class="swatches">
    {
      ansi.map(([token, cls]) => (
        <div class="swatch">
          <span class="swatch__chip" style={`background: var(${token})`} />
          <code>{token}</code>
          <span class="swatch__use">
            <code>.{cls}</code>
          </span>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Type</h2>

  <p>
    A major-third scale, fluid where the jump between a phone and a desk would
    otherwise be jarring.
  </p>

  <div class="scale">
    {
      type.map(([token, use]) => (
        <div class="scale__row">
          <span class="scale__sample" style={`font-size: var(${token})`}>
            The stones are stacked
          </span>
          <code>{token}</code>
          <span class="swatch__use">{use}</span>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Space</h2>

  <p>
    A four-pixel base, doubling loosely. The last two are fluid, because band
    padding is what changes most across viewports.
  </p>

  <div class="space">
    {
      space.map((n) => (
        <div class="space__row">
          <code>--space-{n}</code>
          <span class="space__bar" style={`width: var(--space-${n})`} />
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Components</h2>

  <p>
    Eight, each doing one job. A page composes them; it does not restyle them.
  </p>

  <h3>Button</h3>
  <p class="demo-row">
    <Button href="#components">Primary</Button>
    <Button href="#components" variant="quiet">Quiet</Button>
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>primary</code> is the only filled element on the site and should
    appear once per view.
  </p>

  <h3>Lede</h3>
  <Lede>
    The sentence under a heading, muted and held to a shorter measure so it
    reads as an opening rather than a paragraph.
  </Lede>

  <h3>Callout</h3>
  <Callout>
    A <strong>note</strong> in a document. The rule on the left carries the
    meaning, so there is no icon and no coloured panel.
  </Callout>
  <Callout tone="warn">
    A <strong>warning</strong>. Same shape, different rule.
  </Callout>

  <h3>Band, Grid and Stack</h3>
  <p>
    <code>Band</code> is a horizontal stratum with a tone.
    <code>Grid</code> collapses to one column below a stated minimum.
    <code>Stack</code> owns vertical rhythm, so children never carry margins
    that depend on their neighbours.
  </p>

  <h3>Terminal and Prose</h3>
  <p>
    <code>Terminal</code> renders a recording from
    <code>doc/samples.json</code>; it cannot show anything the program did not
    print. <code>Prose</code> styles whatever a Markdown document emits, so
    writing a document is writing prose.
  </p>

  <style>
    .swatches {
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--space-2);
      margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    }
    .swatch {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 2.5rem 12rem 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-3);
      align-items: center;
      font-size: var(--text-sm);
    }
    .swatch__chip {
      display: block;
      height: 1.5rem;
      border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
      border: var(--hairline);
    }
    .swatch__use {
      color: var(--fg-muted);
    }

    .scale {
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--space-3);
      margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    }
    .scale__row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 9rem 12rem;
      gap: var(--space-3);
      align-items: baseline;
      border-bottom: var(--hairline);
      padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
      font-size: var(--text-sm);
    }
    .scale__sample {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      line-height: var(--leading-tight);
      overflow: hidden;
      text-overflow: ellipsis;
      white-space: nowrap;
    }

    .space {
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--space-2);
      margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    }
    .space__row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 8rem 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-3);
      align-items: center;
      font-size: var(--text-sm);
    }
    .space__bar {
      display: block;
      height: 0.75rem;
      background: var(--accent);
      border-radius: 1px;
    }

    .demo-row {
      display: flex;
      gap: var(--space-5);
      align-items: center;
    }

    @media (max-width: 40rem) {
      .swatch,
      .scale__row,
      .space__row {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
        gap: var(--space-1);
      }
    }
  </style>
</Doc>
